        <h2>Who writes this</h2>
        <p>EarlyLayers is written and run by me, Pratyush Ranjan.</p>
        <p>I started this desk because I found myself spending more time reading original documents than reading crypto news. The most interesting developments in Asian onchain capital markets were often buried in regulatory filings, technical specifications, governance proposals, and local-language announcements that rarely reached English-speaking audiences with their full context.</p>
        <p>I'm based in India, but the desk isn't tied to a geography — it's tied to where the source material leads. Rather than following narratives, I prefer tracing them back to the documents that created them.</p>

        <h2>The method</h2>
        <p>Every piece begins with primary sources.</p>
        <p>My daily workflow is intentionally simple: read official releases, regulatory filings, governance records, technical documentation, corporate disclosures, and whenever necessary, Japanese- and Korean-language material before looking at anyone else's interpretation. Secondary reporting is useful for discovering a story, but it is never treated as evidence on its own.</p>
        <p>The goal isn't to publish first. It's to understand what actually changed, why it matters, and whether the underlying documents support the conclusions people are drawing.</p>

        <h2>Why positions are signed</h2>
        <p>Anyone can summarize documents. Taking a view is where accountability begins.</p>
        <p>Every position is signed and dated because I want readers to know exactly what I believed at a specific point in time. Markets change, information improves, and sometimes a conclusion turns out to be wrong. When that happens, I would rather leave the original view visible, explain what changed, and update the record than quietly rewrite history.</p>
        <p>The purpose of signing positions isn't to appear certain. It's to build a track record that readers can evaluate over time.</p>

        <hr />

        <p>Holdings and conflicts of interest are on the <a href="/disclosure/">disclosure page</a>. For research inquiries, corrections, or feedback: <a href="mailto:research@earlylayers.xyz">research@earlylayers.xyz</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
